-- A refinement written in a `sig` module signature, or in an `extern` FFI
-- signature, enforces nothing -- and as of 2026-07-31 no longer does so
-- silently. Both are the same failure mode `accept/t137` pins for `interface`
-- method signatures: the shape parses, typechecks, and reads exactly like a
-- working contract while obliging no call site and letting no body assume
-- anything.
--
-- `sig`: nothing in `lib/refinecheck` reads `A.sig_def`'s `sig_fns`.
-- `visit_decl` has no `DSig` arm that descends, and a `sig` is an ASCRIPTION
-- -- it constrains what a module exports, not what any particular function
-- body does -- so there is no `fn_def` for the predicate to attach to and no
-- call site that consults it. A MISSING check, not an unsound one. The
-- warning's remedy is the module's own `fn` definition, where a parameter
-- refinement obliges callers and a return refinement is discharged against
-- the body.
--
-- `extern`: the reason is stronger here, and the warning says a DIFFERENT
-- thing on purpose. A refinement on `ef_ret_ty` is not merely unwalked, it is
-- unverifiable in principle -- the callee is foreign C, there is no March body
-- to discharge the claim against, and ASSUMING it would be unsound rather than
-- merely missing. So the remedy cannot be "move it somewhere it gets checked":
-- it is a March wrapper carrying the PARAMETER refinement (where call sites
-- really are obliged), with the foreign RESULT checked at runtime rather than
-- asserted in a type. Reusing `t137`'s "put it on the `impl` method"
-- phrasing here would be actively wrong advice.
--
-- This is an `accept/` witness and its EXIT CODE is the content. A warning
-- does not change `--check`'s status, so this program must stay exit 0.
-- Promoting either shape to a hard error would break code that compiles
-- today, and making a `sig` ascription or an FFI boundary actually ENFORCE a
-- refinement is a much larger question; this witness guards against doing
-- either by accident. The warning TEXT is pinned in
-- `test/test_refinecheck.ml`'s `sig-extern-refinement` suite instead --
-- `check_types.sh` asserts only exit 0 for `accept/`, and pins substrings for
-- `reject/` alone.
--
-- The ENFORCED contrast is deliberately included below: `clamp` is an ordinary
-- top-level `fn` carrying the very same predicate, and there the refinement is
-- real (a violating call is a refinement error). Without it this file would
-- witness only the defect, not the remedy.
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md.
mod T140 do
  needs IO.Foreign

  -- INERT: `sig_fns`' types are never read by the refinement checker.
  sig Store do
    fn put : Int -> {Int | _ > 0}
  end

  -- INERT, and unverifiable in principle: the callee is not March code.
  -- Both positions carry a predicate, since `ef_params` and `ef_ret_ty` are
  -- separate fields and a detector that walked only one would be silent on
  -- the other.
  extern "c" : Cap(IO.Foreign) do
    fn take(n : {Int | _ > 0}) : Int = "take"
    fn count(s : String) : {Int | _ >= 0} = "strlen"
  end

  -- ENFORCED: the same predicate on a real `fn` definition's own parameter.
  -- `clamp(0)` is a refinement ERROR, which is the difference between this
  -- declaration and the two above.
  fn clamp(n : {Int | _ > 0}) : Int do n end

  fn main() : Int do
    clamp(5)
  end
end
